<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Appendix</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<section id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Training Data.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use three preference datasets:</p>
<ul id="A1.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="A1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Skywork Reward Preference 80K</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>: A curated, multi-domain collection of pairwise preferences covering chat, safety, mathematics, and code.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Code-Preference-Pairs</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mejia-Petit, 2024</a>)</cite>: Focuses on fine-grained coding preferences by contrasting correct implementations with systematically perturbed alternatives.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Math-Step-DPO-10K</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lai et al., 2024</a>)</cite>: Provides stepwise preference supervision for mathematical reasoning.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F7" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A1.F7.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2602.20670v1/code_confidence_score_vs_accuracy.png" id="A1.F7.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/204;" width="476" height="204" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F7.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="A1.F7.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Code-Preference-Pairs</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A1.F7.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2602.20670v1/math_confidence_score_vs_accuracy.png" id="A1.F7.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/204;" width="476" height="204" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F7.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="A1.F7.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Math-Step-DPO-10K</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A1.F7.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2602.20670v1/skywork_confidence_score_vs_accuracy.png" id="A1.F7.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/204;" width="476" height="204" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F7.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span><span id="A1.F7.sf3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Skywork-Reward-Preference-80K</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F7.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 7</span>: </span><span id="A1.F7.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Confidence score vs. accuracy on three training datasets using Qwen3-14B.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluation Benchmarks.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A1.I2" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="A1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RewardBench</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lambert et al., 2024</a>)</cite>: An early and widely used benchmark formulated as prompt–chosen–rejected triplets, spanning <em id="A1.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">chat</em>, <em id="A1.I2.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">chat-hard</em>, <em id="A1.I2.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">reasoning</em>, and <em id="A1.I2.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">safety</em>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RM-Bench</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>: Stresses finer-grained preference discrimination and robustness to superficial style cues, covering <em id="A1.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">chat</em>, <em id="A1.I2.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">safety</em>, <em id="A1.I2.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">math</em>, and <em id="A1.I2.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">code</em>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">JudgeBench</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tan et al., 2025</a>)</cite>: Evaluates LLM-based judges on challenging response pairs where preference labels reflect objective correctness rather than crowdsourced stylistic preference.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Baseline Models.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare CAMEL with popular reward models from two paradigms.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Scalar Reward Models.</span>
Scalar reward models map a query–reply pair to a real-valued score and induce pairwise preferences via Bradley–Terry-style modeling.</p>
<ul id="A1.I3" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="A1.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Llama-3-OffsetBias-RM-8B</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al., 2024</a>)</cite>: A Llama-3-based reward model trained with offset bias correction.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ArmoRM-Llama3-8B-v0.1</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>: A multi-objective reward model based on Llama-3 8B.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I3.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Internlm2-20b-reward</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cai et al., 2024</a>)</cite>: A 20B reward model from the InternLM2 family.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I3.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I3.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I3.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I3.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Skywork-Reward-Llama-3.1-8B-v0.2</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>: A reward model trained with bag-of-tricks techniques.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I3.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I3.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I3.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I3.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LDL-Reward-Gemma-2-27B-v0.1<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
                        <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">1</span></span>
                        
                        
                        
                      <a href="https://huggingface.co/ShikaiChen/LDL-Reward-Gemma-2-27B-v0.1" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_medium">https://huggingface.co/ShikaiChen/LDL-Reward-Gemma-2-27B-v0.1</a></span></span></span></span>: A Gemma-2-based reward model with label distribution learning.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I3.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I3.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I3.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I3.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Skywork-Reward-Gemma-2-27B-v0.2</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>: A 27B Gemma-2-based reward model.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I3.i7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I3.i7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I3.i7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I3.i7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Llama-3.1-Nemotron-70B</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>: NVIDIA’s 70B reward model based on Llama-3.1.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I3.i8" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I3.i8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I3.i8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I3.i8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">INF-ORM-Llama3.1-70B</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Minghao Yang, 2024</a>)</cite>: A 70B reward model with improved generalization.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Generative Reward Models.</span>
Generative reward models produce textual judgments before outputting preferences.</p>
<ul id="A1.I4" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="A1.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I4.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GPT-4o</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hurst et al., 2024</a>)</cite>: OpenAI’s multimodal flagship model used as a judge.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I4.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Claude-3.5-Sonnet</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>)</cite>: Anthropic’s Claude 3.5 Sonnet model used as a judge.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I4.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I4.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I4.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I4.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">J1-Llama-8B/70B</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Whitehouse et al., 2025</a>)</cite>: Llama-based judge models trained with reinforcement learning to incentivize thinking.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I4.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I4.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I4.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I4.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RM-R1-Qwen-Instruct-14B/32B</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>: Qwen-based reward models that treat reward modeling as reasoning.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F8" class="ltx_figure">
<div id="A1.F8.3" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:378.5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-34.5pt,37.9pt) scale(0.833335452614982,0.833335452614982) ;"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="A1.F8.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="628.49" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 572.85 628.49" width="572.85"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,628.49) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#0000BF;" fill="#0000BF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 12.99 L 0 615.5 C 0 622.68 5.82 628.49 12.99 628.49 L 559.86 628.49 C 567.03 628.49 572.85 622.68 572.85 615.5 L 572.85 12.99 C 572.85 5.82 567.03 0 559.86 0 L 12.99 0 C 5.82 0 0 5.82 0 12.99 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F9F9F9;" fill="#F9F9F9" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.18 12.99 L 1.18 605.96 L 571.67 605.96 L 571.67 12.99 C 571.67 6.47 566.38 1.18 559.86 1.18 L 12.99 1.18 C 6.47 1.18 1.18 6.47 1.18 12.99 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 20.87 613.77)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:38.38em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="531.07"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A1.F8.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:38.38em;">
<span id="A1.F8.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.F8.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Prompt used for ablation model training.</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 20.87 17.14)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:32.66em;--ltx-fo-height:41.7em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.3em;font-size:10pt;" height="581.16" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 577.01)" width="451.92"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A1.F8.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:32.66em;">
<span id="A1.F8.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.F8.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:120%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">You are an impartial judge evaluating two responses to the same question.<span id="A1.F8.pic1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
Your task is to select the better response based on the following criteria.
<br class="ltx_break">
<br class="ltx_break"><em id="A1.F8.pic1.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Criteria (apply in order):</em>
<br class="ltx_break">1.  </span>Safety<span id="A1.F8.pic1.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">: Immediately eliminate any response that contains harmful content (e.g., discrimination, pornography, dangerous advice) without further comparison.
<br class="ltx_break">2.  </span>Accuracy<span id="A1.F8.pic1.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">: Immediately eliminate any response that contains inaccurate or irresponsible information without further comparison.
<br class="ltx_break">3.  </span>Relevance<span id="A1.F8.pic1.2.1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">: Immediately eliminate any response that is off-topic or fails to address the question without further comparison.
<br class="ltx_break">4.  </span>Comprehensiveness<span id="A1.F8.pic1.2.1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">: Prefer responses that are more complete and cover key aspects of the question.
<br class="ltx_break">5.  </span>Helpfulness<span id="A1.F8.pic1.2.1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">: Prefer responses that are more useful, informative, or actionable.
<br class="ltx_break">
<br class="ltx_break"><em id="A1.F8.pic1.2.1.1.6.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Instructions:</em>
<br class="ltx_break">1. Do not consider response length or order of appearance in your evaluation.
<br class="ltx_break">2. Output only a single verdict: either [[A]] or [[B]].
<br class="ltx_break">
<br class="ltx_break"><em id="A1.F8.pic1.2.1.1.6.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Inputs:</em>
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A1.F8.pic1.2.1.1.6.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#171D91;">&lt;Question&gt;</span>: <span id="A1.F8.pic1.2.1.1.6.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#004A54;">{question}
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A1.F8.pic1.2.1.1.6.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#171D91;">&lt;Response A&gt;</span></span>: <span id="A1.F8.pic1.2.1.1.6.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#004A54;">{ans_a}
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A1.F8.pic1.2.1.1.6.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#171D91;">&lt;Response B&gt;</span></span>: <span id="A1.F8.pic1.2.1.1.6.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#004A54;">{ans_b}</span></span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F8.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 8</span>: </span><span id="A1.F8.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Prompt used for ablation model training.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Ablation Models.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use the prompt shown in Figure <a href="#A1.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ Baseline Models. ‣ A.1 Experimental Setup ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ CAMEL: Confidence-Gated Reflection for Reward Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> to train the ablation model variants. This prompt asks the model to output a single verdict (<span id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[[A]]</span> or <span id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[[B]]</span>) without reflection.</p>
<ul id="A1.I5" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="A1.I5.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I5.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I5.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I5.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Qwen3-14B</span>: The original Qwen3-14B base model without any preference tuning, used as a reference.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I5.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I5.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I5.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I5.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Qwen3-14B-SFT</span>: Supervised fine-tuning on the preference datasets. For each training instance, we construct the input using the prompt in Figure <a href="#A1.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ Baseline Models. ‣ A.1 Experimental Setup ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ CAMEL: Confidence-Gated Reflection for Reward Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> and set the target as the verdict token corresponding to the preferred response (i.e., <span id="A1.I5.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[[A]]</span> if Response A is preferred, <span id="A1.I5.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[[B]]</span> otherwise).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I5.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I5.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I5.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I5.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Qwen3-14B-GRPO</span>: One-epoch GRPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a>)</cite> training starting from Qwen3-14B-SFT, using the same prompt without counterfactual prefix augmentation. The reward is <math id="A1.I5.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+1</annotation></semantics></math> for correct verdicts and <math id="A1.I5.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math> for incorrect ones. To ensure a fair comparison with CAMEL, we double the number of rollouts per instance.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Confidence Score and Accuracy Correlation</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We define the confidence score as the absolute log-probability margin between the two verdict tokens (Eq. <a href="#S3.E5" title="In 3.1 Confidence Score and Accuracy ‣ 3 Method ‣ CAMEL: Confidence-Gated Reflection for Reward Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> in the main text). A higher confidence score indicates that the model strongly favors one option over the other, while a lower score suggests uncertainty.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A1.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ Training Data. ‣ A.1 Experimental Setup ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ CAMEL: Confidence-Gated Reflection for Reward Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> visualizes the relationship between confidence score and prediction accuracy on all three training datasets using Qwen3-14B. Across all datasets, we observe a consistent monotonic relationship: predictions with higher confidence scores are substantially more likely to be correct. This correlation validates our use of the confidence score as an effective proxy for instance difficulty, justifying the confidence-gated reflection mechanism in CAMEL.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</article>
</div>
</div>
